 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "So where are we?",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 1.787
  },
  {
   "text": "What's been certified is the classical Riemann Hypothesis.",
   "start": 1.787,
   "end": 5.288
  },
  {
   "text": "A verification page at MIT reports that the library also proves the generalized",
   "start": 5.288,
   "end": 10.525
  },
  {
   "text": "hypothesis for Dirichlet L-functions.",
   "start": 10.525,
   "end": 13.262
  },
  {
   "text": "The wider generalized hypothesis that the note claims hasn't been checked,",
   "start": 13.262,
   "end": 17.475
  },
  {
   "text": "and there's no human-readable proof yet.",
   "start": 17.475,
   "end": 20.037
  },
  {
   "text": "Alpöge says he intends to understand it, explain it,",
   "start": 20.037,
   "end": 23.35
  },
  {
   "text": "and write it properly.",
   "start": 23.35,
   "end": 24.863
  },
  {
   "text": "The Clay prize requires publication, two years, and general acceptance,",
   "start": 25.3,
   "end": 30.2
  },
  {
   "text": "and Clay's president called the road to real understanding long and difficult.",
   "start": 30.2,
   "end": 34.65
  },
  {
   "text": "So for now, it's a claimed proof: checked by machines,",
   "start": 35.575,
   "end": 39.2
  },
  {
   "text": "not yet understood by people.",
   "start": 39.2,
   "end": 41.2
  },
  {
   "text": "If it holds, the zeros really do all sit on one line,",
   "start": 41.2,
   "end": 44.75
  },
  {
   "text": "and the reason may be an old idea, counting with multiplicity,",
   "start": 44.75,
   "end": 48.5
  },
  {
   "text": "finally made to work for the whole numbers.",
   "start": 48.5,
   "end": 51.0
  }
 ],
 "cues": {
  "certified": 1.787,
  "dirichlet": 5.288,
  "notyet": 13.262,
